  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 1144 PM MDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nThough not as climatologically anomalous as the last couple of\ndays, precipitable water values will remain plenty elevated today,\ncombining with a hair higher instability to promote renewed\nscattered showers and a few generally weak thunderstorms this\nafternoon through the evening. As was the case over the past few\ndays, steering level flow should remain above 20-25 kts, thus\nkeeping storms on the move and limiting overall accumulations for\nmost areas (with some locations, per usual, remaining dry).\nTemperatures will continue to hold 5-10 degrees above normal for\nthe date.\n\nSaturday remains the day to watch in terms of both severe threats\nand localized flooding potential. Despite a small downtick in\navailable moisture, PWs will remain elevated, with moderately \ndeep warm cloud depths and increased instability as cool air \nadvection returns aloft with the approaching upper-level low.\nSteering level flow will continue to be hefty, but improved shear\nand higher coverage of late day convection will lead to a greater\npotential for heavy downpours namely over the plains. The\nexception to the above trend will be the high country, where drier\nair will steadily gain influence through the weekend and reduce\nprecipitation coverage and intensity. Scattered afternoon showers\nand thunderstorms look to continue into Sunday with the passage of\nthe upper-level trough and sufficient shear and instability,\nhowever a more noteworthy decline in PWs and cooler surface\nconditions would suggest a slight reduction in activity compared\nto Saturday.\n\nTransient ridging emerges by Monday, providing for a drier day\nacross most of the region with afternoon showers becoming much\nmore isolated in nature, albeit not disappearing entirely.\nTemperatures will be fairly seasonal if not a touch below average,\nwith highs in the mid 70's Monday for the lower elevations.\n\nSouthwest flow aloft will dominate past Monday, allowing for the \nintrusion of a few shortwaves through the week and keeping \nopportunities for additional precipitation in place most days, \nwith the mountains being best positioned for the better coverage given\nthe flow pattern. Beyond that, there's still ample variance in \nmodel guidance, limiting how much detail we can extract.",
